        "role": "Manually derived case account with firing and invariance controls",
        "encoding": "utf-8",
        "text": "These are static predictions from supplied text, not test outputs or executed dictionaries. Use ENTRIES.json's session_record, collected_head of 40 e characters, collection_name='hypothetical-collection', and each file's own listed expected_digest. Read results are stipulated to supply exact bytes and matching digest metadata.\n\nreport-valid.md:\n  completion.value='complete'; report_kind.value='answer'.\n  Both validation objects have status='valid', the full parsed declaration, six comparisons with matches=true, and diagnostics=[]. The five session comparisons and the collection-HEAD comparison have distinct source labels as described in route-and-sites.\n\nreport-mismatch.md:\n  Both claim values are null. validation.status='mismatch'.\n  validation.declaration still says completion='complete', report_kind='answer', target_commit=40 f characters.\n  validation.comparisons.target_commit says expected=40 a characters, observed=40 f characters, source='pinned session', matches=false. The other five comparisons match. The diagnostic has reason='identity_mismatch' and field='target_commit'.\n  This is the firing case for the identity comparison. Paired with report-valid.md, only the declared target_commit differs; both strings are well-formed commit IDs. Thus a target-format failure is not the explanation for the differing classification, and retaining raw claim values is not mistaken for validating their binding.\n\nreport-missing.md:\n  Both claim values are null. validation.status='missing'; declaration=null; comparisons={}; a diagnostic has reason='missing'. The prose says work is complete but does not produce a completion value. The source still identifies collection hypothetical-collection REPORT.md because these report bytes were stipulated to be read.\n\nreport-invalid.md:\n  Both claim values are null. validation.status='invalid'; declaration=null; comparisons={}; a diagnostic has reason='duplicate_key', for completion.\n  The repeated completion values partial and complete are not resolved by choosing either one. This is a firing case for structural invalidity. Its null claim values are the same as report-missing.md, but the full returned dictionaries distinguish invalid from missing. The invalid view does not preserve both competing completion values as structured claims; recovering arbitrary invalid text is outside the stated use.\n\nreport-partial-null.md:\n  completion.value='partial'; report_kind.value=null. validation.status='valid'; validation.declaration.report_kind is explicitly null; all six identity comparisons match.\n  Null here is a validated allowed declaration value, unlike null produced by missing, invalid or mismatch. As a further source-only deduction, deleting the report_kind member from these bytes would trigger missing_field at report_declaration.py:110-111 rather than produce this valid/null result. That variant was not created or executed.\n\nreport-prose-variant.md:\n  Its declaration is identical to report-valid.md; only surrounding commentary differs. Under the same name, session and HEAD, with each report's own correct expected digest, its returned claim and validation content is identical to report-valid.md. The digest is checked but not included in the returned dictionary. This is the invariance case: prose changes do not change a structured completion or report-kind claim.\n\nAll six cases retain the claim-level auditor attribution and non-process/non-judgment note. Equal top-level claim values do not suffice to compare preservation; the differing validation operands and context must be inspected."
